 with E6 and J6, and the quiet endgame held Black's narrow lead. Over the game GPT-6.1 Sol lost 5.7 points per move with 9 blunders, against 6.1 and 13 for the 20k.", "moments": [{"turn": 9, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's E2 slid from -0.8 to -6.0, losing 5.1 points. The engine preferred E7. It was the first sizable slip in an otherwise even opening."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "With the lead at -0.3, F2 lost 14.8 points and dropped Black to -15.1. The engine wanted E8. The bottom was getting crowded with Black stones while White's wall kept growing."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "The 20k's E4 lost 21.1 points, turning Black's lead from -11.2 to +9.9. The engine's choice was E6. A game that looked lost for Black was suddenly winning."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "The game's biggest mistake came right after. At +23.6, GPT-6.1 Sol played C2 and fell to -16.6, a loss of 40.2 points. The engine's choice was E6, the point White later took on move 46."}, {"turn": 46, "text": "At -19.7 Black looked beaten, but the 20k's E6 lost 17.4 points and put the lead at -2.3. The engine preferred H8. White's next move, J6, lost another 6.3 and Black went ahead at +4.0. That decided the game."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every script only listed tools, played the single move the player named, parsed the returned board into groups, liberties and empty regions (the stored inspect11 function), stored notes and logs, counted final area, built the SGF, and read or wrote files. Nothing looped over candidate moves, scored points, or played any continuation forward, so move choice was entirely the player's own.", "stop": false}}